\title{A survey of how far extraction reaches}
\taxon{Change}
\date{2026-11-23}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{changelog}
\tag{extraction}
\tag{tooling}
\meta{pr}{6}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\code{\verb~|scripts/survey-extraction.py~} compiles every \code{\verb~|float~} and \code{\verb~|_Float16~} entrypoint of the math library of LLVM libc and nine double-precision ones as \code{\verb~|extract/extract.sh~} does, runs \code{\verb~|ir2lean~} on the function that holds the body, and lists every construct outside the exported fragment. It takes about ten seconds. \pr{6}.}
\ul{
\li{The result is the note [How far extraction reaches](note-extraction-coverage): 27 of 96 single-precision entrypoints export, none of 88 half-precision and none of 9 double-precision ones. What is missing is [prob-IW37](prob-IW37).}
\li{Three defects of the tools were found on the way: [prob-ZWH4](prob-ZWH4), [prob-HVD8](prob-HVD8), [prob-JYU7](prob-JYU7).}
\li{The pull request first held the result as \code{\verb~|docs/extraction-coverage.md~}. In review it was moved into this journal without change of content, split into five trees, and \code{\verb~|scripts/md2tree.py~} was added to convert the Markdown that the script prints. The journal gained the kind \code{\verb~|Note~} for such documents, listed under [notes](notes). Session [ses-DARM](ses-DARM).}
}
